= Using the bignumber class =

The [../classes/html/classbignumber.html bignumber class] provides an arbitrary-precision signed integer.  There is no practical limit on the magnitude of a bignumber, other than the amount of memory available.

Unlike most rudiments classes, bignumber is a value type, like an int, rather than a stateful object.  Instances may be freely copied, assigned, and passed by value.

Instances can be constructed from a decimal or hexadecimal string, from the native integer types, or from a raw big-endian byte buffer, and setValue() and the assignment operators can set an existing instance to any of these forms as well.  getString() and getString(base) convert back to a decimal or hexadecimal string, getValue() converts back to a native integer type (failing if the value doesn't fit), and getMagnitude() and related methods expose the raw magnitude and sign.

Arithmetic follows the same rules as the native C++ integer types: division truncates toward zero, and the remainder takes the sign of the dividend.  So, for example, -7/2 is -3 and -7%2 is -1, while 7/-2 is -3 and 7%-2 is 1.  The full set of arithmetic, comparison, unary, and bitwise/shift operators is provided (+ - * / % == != < > <= >= unary - ++ -- & | ^ ~ << >>), along with named methods (add(), subtract(), compare(), and so on) that the operators are built on.

The bitwise operators (&, |, ^, and ~) behave as if each operand were an infinitely wide two's complement value, which is the same behavior that Java's !BigInteger and Python's int provide.  In particular, ~x is defined as -x-1.

bignumber also provides a family of modular arithmetic methods - nonNegativeModulo(), modAdd(), modSub(), modMul(), modPow(), modInverse(), and the related gcd() - useful for implementing public-key protocols like Diffie-Hellman, RSA, and SRP.  Unlike modulo() and operator%(), which take the sign of the dividend, these methods always give a non-negative result, in the range 0 through the modulus minus 1.  For example, modulo() on -7 with 3 gives -1, while nonNegativeModulo() on -7 with 3 gives 2.  modPow() computes modular exponentiation efficiently, without ever materializing the full, unreduced power, and modInverse() computes the modular multiplicative inverse, failing when none exists.

Operations that can fail - parsing a malformed string, dividing by zero, or converting a value that's too large to a native integer type - don't throw exceptions.  They report failure through their return value, and getError() returns the reason.

When available, this class uses !OpenSSL's BIGNUM implementation internally.  Otherwise it falls back to a built-in implementation, so isSupported() always returns true.

